# --- registry -------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by setup/lib/models.sh; passed to llama-server as environment and
# ignored there. MODEL_SWA=yes means the profile MUST carry --swa-full —
# without it every Claude Code turn re-processes the whole prompt.
MODEL_TITLE=Qwen3.6-35B-A3B · Q8, 35.8 GiB · 1.6-4.6x flashnext's decode and ~2.9x its prefill at 40.8 GiB of GTT against 91.0 (04.09.2026) — speed measured, QUALITY NOT, and published benchmarks put the model BELOW flashnext
# MODEL_SWA=no — MEASURED, not assumed: config.json of Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B
# (fetched 04.09.2026) has no `sliding_window` key at all. What it has instead
# is `layer_types`, 40 entries alternating three `linear_attention` and one
# `full_attention` (`full_attention_interval: 4`). Linear attention is not a
# sliding window — it keeps a fixed-size recurrent state per sequence rather
# than a capped window of tokens — so --swa-full has nothing to do here.
MODEL_SWA=no
# MODEL_SOURCE — where this model comes from, so the profile can fetch it.
# `<repo> <pattern>…`: every pattern is a substring of a path in that repo and
# every matching .gguf is fetched. Verified 04.09.2026 against the repo's own
# tree listing, by matching the exact filename this profile's -m points at —
# not by guessing.
#
# The mmproj is NOT in this list yet, and the reason is a defect rather than a
# decision: this repo's model directory is one flat namespace, and unsloth
# names the vision encoder after its PRECISION, not after its model. So
# `mmproj-F16.gguf` here (899,283,680 B) and `mmproj-F16.gguf` for qwen38
# (927,607,488 B, on this disk since 17.08.) are the same path. Adding the
# pattern would have fetch-model.sh resume into another profile's file.
# Measured 04.09.2026; see the open points at the end of this file.
MODEL_SOURCE=unsloth/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF UD-Q8_K_XL
#
# THE QUANT IS Q8, AND THAT IS A MEASURED DECISION RATHER THAN THE USUAL ONE.
# Every other profile here serves Q4_K_XL, because the received wisdom is that
# decode on this machine is bandwidth-bound and doubling the weights halves it.
# MEASURED 04.09.2026 on this model, one variable, same window, same flags,
# same build — bench/reports/2026-09-04_1048_speed_qwen36-q8-rocm against
# …_0937_speed_qwen36-ngram-pmin075:
#
#                    Q4      Q8     Q8/Q4
#     prose d512    35.2    33.4     0.95
#     prose d36k    29.5    27.5     0.93
#     count d36k   161.6   155.3     0.96
#     copy  d8192  281.5   258.9     0.92
#     prefill d512 683.3   635.5     0.93
#     prefill d36k 662.1   609.5     0.92
#
# **Q8 costs 5-8 %, not half.** The wisdom is not wrong in general; it does not
# reach this model. Only 3B of 35B parameters are active per token, so the
# weight traffic per token is small enough that it is not what decode waits
# for here — and with speculation accepting 100 % on count and copy, several
# tokens come out of one forward pass anyway.
#
# WHY THAT DECIDES THE PROFILE. The open question about this model is not
# speed, it is QUALITY: the published agentic-coding figures put it below the
# incumbent, and 3B active against 6B is why. Against that, spending 5-8 % so
# that the quantisation adds nothing further is the obvious trade. Q8_K_XL is
# the closest thing to the released checkpoint short of BF16 — which this
# machine must not run (an operator handover reports BF16 collapsing to ~25 %
# of Q8 on Strix Halo; not measured here and not worth measuring).
#
# The price is memory, and it is affordable: GTT 40.83 GiB against Q4's 25.84,
# still well under half of what flashnext pins (91.0). The Q4 file stays on
# disk so the comparison above can be re-run without a download.
# TEMPLATE_LEVELS — which reasoning levels this model's chat template renders.
# `no-levels` is the MEASURED answer, not the default: the template shipped
# inside the GGUF (read 04.09.2026 out of the Hugging Face API's `gguf`
# metadata, 8,057 chars) contains `enable_thinking` and `preserve_thinking`
# and nothing else — no `reasoning_effort`, no `thinking_budget`. So thinking
# is a switch here, not a dial, and a mode that named a level would be silently
# dropped by the template. setup/gateway/modes.py refuses one instead.
TEMPLATE_LEVELS=no-levels
# Every vocabulary word maps to the one thing the template understands. The
# same shape gemma26 and batch use, and for the same reason.
MODES=low:on  medium:on  high:on  xhigh:on  max:on
# MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN — what one token of context costs in KV, MEASURED by
# the two-point method: load at -c 65536 and at -c 262144 and take the GTT
# difference, so the weights and the compute buffers cancel out of the
# subtraction rather than having to be estimated.
MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN=21.0
MODEL_KV_SOURCE=04.09.2026, two-point under bench/sideserver.py on build-rocm-patched-b10702-11-gc799f1014: the server's own GTT (per DRM client) read 21.90 GiB at -c 65536 and 25.84 at -c 262144, i.e. 3.94 GiB over 196,608 tokens = 21.01 KiB per token, 5.25 GiB at the full window. The -c 262144 point is bench/reports/2026-09-04_0924_speed_qwen36-c262144; the -c 65536 point ran budget.py --observe and nothing else, so it produced NO report of its own and both raw outputs are kept verbatim in bench/reports/2026-09-04_0940_qwen36-vs-flashnext/memory-observations.txt. The estimate derived from the checkpoint's config beforehand was 20.0 — 5 % low, which is the one time on this machine such a derivation has come close (flashnext's was 56 % low).
# MODEL_GTT_BASE_GIB — what GTT shows with the KV taken out, i.e. the weights
# the GPU really pins plus its compute buffers. Computed from EACH of the two
# points above independently, and they agree to the second decimal: 21.90 -
# 1.31 = 20.59, and 25.84 - 5.25 = 20.59. That agreement is the check, not a
# coincidence — it is what says the model is linear in the window and that
# neither point was taken in a disturbed state.
#
# NOTE it is BELOW the 20.82 GiB file size. So about 0.23 GiB of the file is
# never pinned. Not investigated; it is the harmless direction (the guard
# charges more than the model takes) and it is what the two-point method is
# for. Do not "correct" it upwards to the file size.
#
# THE FIGURE BELOW IS Q8's AND IT IS ONE POINT, NOT TWO. The profile serves
# Q8 (see MODEL_SOURCE), so the base had to move with it: GTT 40.83 GiB
# observed at -c 262144, minus 21.0 KiB/token x 262144 = 5.25 GiB of KV,
# gives 35.58 — against a 35.81 GiB file, the same small shortfall Q4 showed.
# Only one window was loaded at Q8, so this rests on the KV RATE from the Q4
# two-point above rather than on its own pair. That rate is quant-independent
# (the KV is f16 either way) and the Q8 run confirmed it from its own side:
# `budget.py --observe` read 20.07 KiB/token there, the same value to the
# second decimal as the Q4 run. A second Q8 window would still be the
# stronger evidence, and nobody has loaded one.
MODEL_GTT_BASE_GIB=35.58
# MODEL_HOST_ANON_GIB — what stays resident OUTSIDE GTT. RssAnon of the
# RUNNING server, the one-line check this repo requires: 0.24 GiB at Q4/-c
# 65536, 0.25 at Q4/-c 262144, 0.27 at Q8/-c 262144 — three loads, all on the
# binary LLAMA_BIN names below, and the figure barely moves with either the
# window or the quant. The declared value is the largest of them. Re-measure
# if that pin ever moves: 28.6 GiB was found where 0.31 was declared on
# 31.08., which is how this rule got written.
MODEL_HOST_ANON_GIB=0.27
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHY THIS MODEL IS HERE
# ----------------------
# The operator asked for a FASTER model at sufficient quality — flashnext is
# the incumbent and it is large: 103.7 GiB of file, 80.8 GiB measured in GTT.
# This one is 35.8 GiB at the Q8 this profile settled on, and the interesting
# number was never the file size but the ACTIVE parameter count: 3B of 35B per
# token against flashnext's 6B. That is what decode time is made of here — and
# it is also, on the published figures, why this model is the weaker coder.
# One fact, both consequences.
#
# THE HYPOTHESIS HELD, AND THE QUANT MEASUREMENT IS WHY IT MATTERS. Decode was
# expected to be weight-bandwidth-bound, which would have made Q8 cost half
# the speed; measured, it costs 5-8 % (see MODEL_SOURCE). So the fast model
# can be run at near-checkpoint fidelity, and the remaining quality question
# is about the MODEL and not about how it is stored here.
#
# Not everything in the paragraph above was measured here — the file size is read
# off the disk, everything else comes from the model's own config.
#
# WHAT IT IS, from the checkpoint's own config.json (fetched 04.09.2026 from
# Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B; every figure below is a READ, not a measurement):
#
#     num_hidden_layers          40
#     layer_types                30 x linear_attention, 10 x full_attention
#     full_attention_interval    4        (every 4th layer keeps a real KV)
#     num_key_value_heads        2
#     head_dim                   256
#     num_experts                256, num_experts_per_tok 8
#     moe_intermediate_size      512, shared_expert_intermediate_size 512
#     max_position_embeddings    262144
#     mtp_num_hidden_layers      1        (see SPECULATION below)
#     vocab_size                 248320
#
# THE ARCHITECTURE TAG SAYS qwen35, AND THAT IS NOT A MISTAKE. The GGUF
# declares `general.architecture = qwen35moe` and the checkpoint declares
# `model_type: qwen3_5_moe` — Qwen3.6 reuses Qwen3.5's architecture and
# changes the weights and the post-training, not the graph. Consequence, and
# it is the good one: **no new llama.cpp build is needed.** Checked 04.09.2026
# against the built artifact rather than the source, because a tree and a
# binary are different things — `strings bin/libllama.so | grep '^qwen35moe$'`
# hits in build-rocm-patched (b10702-11-gc799f1014) and in the pinned
# cachelookup build alike.
#
# WHAT ONE TOKEN OF CONTEXT COSTS — DERIVED, NOT MEASURED. Written down so the
# measurement has something to disagree with, and marked so nobody mistakes it
# for the real thing:
#
#     10 full-attention layers x 2 KV heads x 256 head_dim
#       = 5,120 elements for K, the same for V
#     10,240 elements x 2 B (f16) = 20,480 B
#     -> 20.0 KiB per token, i.e. 5.0 GiB at -c 262144
#
# plus a per-SEQUENCE constant for the 30 linear layers (conv + SSM state,
# ~2 MB per layer at float32, ~60 MB total) which does not grow with the
# window. For scale: flashnext measured 37.3 KiB/token, qwen38 74.3.
# THE ESTIMATE MADE FOR FLASHNEXT BEFORE ITS DOWNLOAD WAS 56 % LOW. Treat
# this one the same way — it is arithmetic over a config file, and the config
# file does not know what llama.cpp allocates.
#
# --- the arguments, and what is and is not known about each -----------------
#
# -c 262144: the model's own max_position_embeddings, and CHECKED against the
#   measured KV figure rather than the estimate — 21.0 KiB/token x 262,144 =
#   5.25 GiB, which is what the observed GTT shows. The full window is
#   affordable here in a way it has never been on this machine: flashnext
#   pays 37.3 KiB/token for the same thing and had to be cut to 240,640.
# -np 1: NOT a fresh decision. Two independent defects were measured on this
#   hardware at two slots (setup/env/qwen38.env carries the matrix): the
#   gfx1151 HIP race and slot-restore poisoning. Neither is model-specific —
#   both are properties of this GPU and this server — so a new model does not
#   get to reopen the question by default.
# -fa on -fit off -ub 512 -b 2048: the house shape. Every one of these was
#   MEASURED — but on qwen38 and on flashnext, NOT on this model. -ub 512
#   beat 2048 by 25 % at d32768 on qwen38 (bench/reports/
#   2026-08-31_1724_ub-sweep) and by 11 % at d0 on flashnext
#   (…_2018_flashnext-ub); -fit off is not a tuning at all but a refusal to
#   let the fitter guess (flashnext.env carries the crash report it comes
#   from). Carried here as the best available starting point and NOT as a
#   finding about Qwen3.6. The sweep is what turns them into one.
# -cram 32768: RAM-backed prompt cache, same as qwen38. Cheap here in a way
#   it is not for flashnext — the weights are 35.8 GiB rather than 103.7, so
#   the host side has room it has never had before. NOT measured; -cram is a
#   ceiling, and what it actually buys is a separate question
#   (setup/README.md, "What -cram buys").
# --chat-template-kwargs {"enable_thinking":false}: thinking OFF by default,
#   with MODES above able to turn it on per request. Same arrangement as
#   qwen38 and flashnext.
#
# SPECULATION: the N-GRAM DRAFTER, and NO -md. The checkpoint HAS an MTP head
# (mtp_num_hidden_layers: 1 in its config), but unsloth converts with
# --no-nextn and ships GGUFs without it — confirmed on the file itself
# (gguf_dump --no-tensors, 04.09.2026: not one key matching nextn or mtp), the
# same reason flashnext needs a separately exported head (unsloth discussion
# #47). So with THIS file `-md` has nothing to point at, and what is left is
# the drafter that needs no second model — which turns out to be enough to
# beat the incumbent on every cell.
#
#   It is not the ceiling, though, and the open points at the end say where
#   the ceiling might be: a GGUF that carries the head would give the one
#   thing an n-gram drafter cannot, which is help on PROSE — its acceptance
#   there is 0-14 % because an n-gram drafts from the prompt and novel text
#   is not in it.
#
#   MEASURED 04.09.2026, three sideserver runs at the profile's own window,
#   one variable each, same three workloads at 512/8192/32768. Decode, t/s:
#
#                       nospec            ngram-mod       + p-min 0.75
#     prose   d512       35.7               35.4              35.2
#     prose   d8192      34.2               32.2              44.2
#     prose   d36k       30.6               28.3              29.5
#     count   d512       35.4              197.9             202.9
#     count   d8192      34.2              188.3             219.7
#     count   d36k       30.7              157.4             161.6
#     copy    d512       36.2              113.2             291.3
#     copy    d8192      34.2              268.0             281.5
#     copy    d36k       30.7               94.6             220.3
#
#   bench/reports/2026-09-04_{0924_speed_qwen36-c262144,0934_speed_qwen36-ngram,
#   0937_speed_qwen36-ngram-pmin075}.
#
#   WHAT DECIDES IT IS THE COPY COLUMN, and that is not a preference. `copy`
#   is a block reproduced with one substitution — the shape of most of what a
#   coding agent emits — and it is the workload an n-gram drafter can help,
#   because an n-gram drafts FROM THE PROMPT. `count` is predictable but
#   absent from the prompt; `prose` is the floor no drafter reaches. Reading
#   only one of them is how this repo once recorded "the ngram drafters give
#   nothing" from a probe that could not have shown one working
#   (bench/README.md).
#
#   --spec-draft-p-min 0.75 is what keeps prose from paying for it. Without
#   it the drafter also runs where it cannot win (acceptance 3.9 % at d8192,
#   0.0 % at d36k) and prose loses 1-7 %; with it, acceptance on prose rises
#   to 7-14 % and the column is level with nospec. Same value flashnext
#   carries and for the same stated reason — but measured here rather than
#   copied, which is the difference this file is for.
#
#   HOW MUCH OF THIS TO BELIEVE. Six of the eighteen cells carry speed.py's
#   own spread warning (copy at d512 ranged 87.3-294.3 t/s over three reps),
#   and decode on this machine has a 19 % coefficient of variation, so
#   nothing under ~22 % is resolvable at n=3. The DIRECTIONS here are 2-8x
#   and safely outside that; the individual numbers are not. The one cell to
#   distrust is prose d8192 = 44.2, which is above the nospec figure it
#   should at best have matched — read it as "no loss", not as a gain.
#
LLAMA_ARGS=--alias qwen36 -m @MODELS@/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-Q8_K_XL.gguf \
  --spec-type ngram-mod --spec-draft-n-max 4 --spec-ngram-mod-n-min 24 \
  --spec-draft-p-min 0.75 \
  -ngl 999 -fa on -fit off -ub 512 -b 2048 -c 262144 -np 1 -cram 32768 \
  --chat-template-kwargs {"enable_thinking":false} \
  --slot-save-path @HOME@/.cache/llama-slots \
  --jinja --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8080
# Backend: ROCm, via the unpinned `build-rocm-patched` symlink rather than an
# explicit build id — flashnext pins a foreign lineage and owes a retirement
# condition for it (CLAUDE.md); a new model has no reason to inherit that debt.
#
# MEASURED 04.09.2026 and the answer is SPLIT, which is a result rather than a
# failure to decide (bench/README.md: "a configuration that wins one column and
# loses the other"). bench/variants/qwen36.json, 2x2 at -c 65536,
# platform_profile=performance verified unchanged at both ends of the run —
# bench/reports/2026-09-04_0942_sweep_qwen36. Decode, t/s:
#
#                    prose            count            copy
#                d512 8k  36k    d512  8k   36k    d512  8k   36k
#   rocm-nospec   38.1 35.3 30.8   47.6 35.2 31.6   36.7 34.3 30.7
#   vulkan-nospec 55.1 51.5 44.9   54.1 51.3 44.9   54.0 51.3 45.0
#   rocm-ngram    39.0 39.4 36.0  223.3 212.2 178.2 105.3 285.6 233.0
#   vulkan-ngram  57.0 48.8 41.4  213.0 203.4 172.6 108.9 226.8 112.1
#
#   WITHOUT SPECULATION VULKAN WINS BY 1.45x, and that half is solid: the
#   factor repeats to two decimals at all three depths (1.45/1.46/1.46),
#   neither cell carries a spread warning, and 45 % is twice this machine's
#   ~22 % resolution floor. Prefill is a tie at d512 and Vulkan +16 % at d36k.
#
#   WITH THE DRAFTER THE ORDER INVERTS ON THE COLUMN THAT MATTERS. `copy` is
#   the shape of most of what a coding agent emits, and at depth ROCm takes
#   it — 285.6 against 226.8 at d8192, 233.0 against 112.1 at d36k. It is
#   also the more trustworthy half: neither ROCm cell carries a spread
#   warning, while the Vulkan d36k copy cell ranged 90.1-197.8 t/s, so its
#   median is not a number. Vulkan keeps `prose` (41.4 against 36.0) and
#   prefill (748.1 against 669.5).
#
#   SO ROCM STAYS, on the workload the machine is actually for — and this is
#   the weakest-held line in this file. Two things handicap the Vulkan arm and
#   BOTH understate it:
#     * build-vulkan is commit 54ee5ee of 22.08., against the ROCm arm's
#       b10702-11-gc799f1014 of 30.08. Eight days of upstream, on a stack
#       where a build difference has been worth 25 % before.
#     * the RADV APU heap option is NOT set on this machine — there is no
#       drirc anywhere (checked 04.09.2026; Mesa is 26.1.7, so the option
#       exists). Strix Halo operators report it as required for Vulkan on a
#       unified-memory APU. Setting it changes GPU memory behaviour for every
#       Vulkan client including the desktop, on a machine that has frozen
#       three times over GTT — an operator decision, not a repo one.
#   The follow-up that would settle it: rebuild Vulkan on the ROCm arm's
#   commit, set the driconf option, and re-run the two ngram cells through
#   bench/suites/decode-rate.py (seven rounds, not three) — the instrument
#   that exists because a three-round mean once read a 12 % regression that
#   was not there.
LLAMA_BIN=llama.cpp/build-rocm-patched/bin/llama-server
#
# --- open points, in the order they block something -------------------------
#
#   [x] MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN, MODEL_GTT_BASE_GIB, MODEL_HOST_ANON_GIB —
#       done 04.09.2026, two-point, both points agreeing on the base.
#   [x] Is it actually FASTER than flashnext? Yes, and by more than the
#       hypothesis expected: at the Q8 this profile serves, decode 1.6-4.6x
#       and prefill ~2.9x on every one of the nine cells, at 41.4 GiB of GTT
#       against flashnext's 91.0. (At Q4 it is 1.8-4.8x — the 5-8 % the quant
#       costs, see MODEL_SOURCE.)
#       bench/reports/2026-09-04_0927_speed_flashnext-live-04.09 is the
#       incumbent's half, taken the same morning on the machine's own
#       production shape rather than read out of an older report.
#   [x] `--spec-type ngram-mod`: measured, kept, see the table above.
#
#   [ ] IS THE QUALITY SUFFICIENT — THE ONLY QUESTION LEFT, AND THE ONE THIS
#       PROFILE CANNOT ANSWER. Nothing above is a quality statement. The
#       published figures point the other way: on agentic coding benchmarks
#       Qwen3.6-35B-A3B is BELOW the incumbent — SWE-bench Multilingual 67.2
#       against Flash-Next's 81.0, SWE-bench Pro 49.5 against 62.5 (vendor
#       and third-party numbers, read 04.09.2026, NOT measured here and not
#       comparable across sources without care). 3B active parameters against
#       6B is exactly why it is fast, and plausibly also why it is weaker.
#       This is an operator judgement on real work, not a benchmark this repo
#       can run — see the note at the top of bench/README.md for why the
#       model battery was removed rather than repaired.
#   [ ] What this repo CAN still say about it: suites/depth-correctness.py
#       (does it stay right as the window fills — a property of the build)
#       and the gateway's passive degeneracy check on real traffic. Neither
#       has run against this model.
#   [~] The BACKEND: measured 04.09.2026 and SPLIT — see the LLAMA_BIN block.
#       ROCm stays on the copy column; Vulkan wins the unspeculated floor by
#       1.45x while handicapped by an older build and a missing driver
#       option. Not closed, and the named follow-up is in that block.
#   [ ] -ub 512, -b 2048, -cram 32768 are carried from other models and
#       unmeasured here. A Strix Halo handover the operator brought on
#       04.09. recommends -b 4096 -ub 4096 for this model, which is the
#       OPPOSITE of what this repo measured on qwen38 (-ub 512 beat 2048 by
#       25 % at d32768) — but that was a different model and their figure
#       comes from llama-bench rather than from a serving profile. One
#       flag-ab run settles it; until then neither number is this model's.
#   [ ] q8_0 KV. Same handover reports +151 % at 64k. This repo's objection
#       to it is a CORRECTNESS one — q8 KV dropped the long-context task on
#       qwen38 (docs/HANDOVER.md, "Do not try again") — so the speed claim
#       cannot be acted on before that question is re-asked on this model.
#   [x] A LARGER QUANT — measured and adopted. UD-Q8_K_XL costs 5-8 %, not
#       half; the whole account is under MODEL_SOURCE. Note this is the one
#       decision in the file that a change of PRIORITY would reverse rather
#       than a change of evidence: if maximum throughput matters more than
#       fidelity, Q4 is 5-8 % faster and the file is still on disk.
#
#   AND ONE THING THAT MUST NOT BE COPIED FROM THAT HANDOVER: it launches
#   with `-np 3 --kv-unified`. Two slots on this GPU are a MEASURED defect
#   here, not a preference — the gfx1151 HIP race (llama.cpp #27579, root
#   cause #27572) turns every answer into '////', and slot restore with two
#   slots was corrupt 4/4 with a populated store. The matrix is in
#   setup/env/qwen38.env. -np 1 stays until somebody re-measures it.
#   [~] AN MTP HEAD — the sidecar route is CLOSED, the other one is open and
#       is the largest remaining throughput lever. havenoammo's sidecar
#       (35BA3B-MTP.gguf) cannot be a draft model: it is missing ten metadata
#       keys AND `token_embd.weight`, and only the metadata is repairable.
#       Registered as `qwen36-sidecar-mtp-head-has-no-embeddings`, with the
#       one-line check that answers it in seconds.
#       WHAT REMAINS: the same publisher's FULL models carry the MTP layer
#       inside the main GGUF (`Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-MTP-UD-*.gguf`), used as -m
#       with `--spec-type draft-mtp` and no -md — the shape qwen38 serves.
#       Verified from that file's first 16 MB rather than by downloading it:
#       753 tensors, `nextn_predict_layers 1`, `block_count 41`, four
#       `blk.40.nextn.*`, `token_embd.weight` present.
#       AND THE CLEAN VERSION OF THE SAME THING: convert it ourselves.
#       `conversion/qwen.py:636` registers this exact architecture and its
#       chain carries `supports_mtp_export = True`, so the local converter
#       can keep the head that unsloth drops with `--no-nextn`. That buys
#       known provenance instead of a third-party quant with no imatrix;
#       it costs unsloth's UD recipe, which matters little at Q8 and a great
#       deal at Q4. Neither the conversion nor the serving has been tried.
#   [ ] VISION — mmproj blocked on the filename collision described under
#       MODEL_SOURCE until 04.09.2026; the fetcher now refuses rather than
#       destroys, so the remaining question is only which precision to fetch
#       under which name.
